Namespace platformWindow, because Qt defines it too and means something else entirely.

THE COLLISION. Cocotron NSWindow keeps its backend object behind -platformWindow, and every
internal call site asks for it by that name. Qt subclasses NSWindow as QNSWindow and ALSO defines
-platformWindow, returning its C++ QCocoaWindow pointer, which is not an object at all. A subclass
method wins, so every [window platformWindow] inside AppKit reached QT and handed back a C++
object, whose first word is a vtable pointer. objc_msgSend read that vtable as an isa.

CMake.app died the first time -orderWindow:relativeTo: ordered its main window. The evidence:

  CIDER_WIN orderAbove self=... class=QNSWindow platformWindow=0x0 relativeTo=0
  CIDER_APP messaging receiver=0x... (placeAboveWindow:) isa=0x... class=unreadable (in an image)

The ivar was NIL and the returned pointer was NOT, and our getter never printed its own trace, so
the method that ran was not ours. class=unreadable IN AN IMAGE is a vtable, not a class object.

AppKit now asks for cider_platformWindow, which no application defines. 30 sends across six files.
-platformWindow stays as a forwarder for anything outside AppKit, but AppKit itself must never use
it again: a subclass can and does override it.

FOUR HYPOTHESES DIED FIRST, and one of them was mine to begin with: I had added two ivars to
NSWindow in the previous commit for window tabbing, and adding an ivar shifts the layout every
subclass sits on. That was a real risk and I tested it rather than argue it, by moving tabbing to
associated objects. THE CRASH WAS IDENTICAL, so the ivars were innocent. The associated object
version is kept anyway, because a compatibility shim is not worth a layout change to a class every
toolkit subclasses.

ALSO, THE BACKING STORE CONVERSIONS. NSView had none of the six 10.7 methods, and Qt asks
-convertSizeToBacking: on every QNSView. The whole family goes in together, written against the
window backing scale rather than hard coded to 1, because a caller that converts one way converts
back and finding them one exception at a time is what this already cost.

RESULT: CMake.app CREATES ITS REAL MAIN WINDOW for the first time, 614x506 at 308,126 style 0xf.
It still paints nothing and still dies, now in objc_msgSend +0x1d sending release to a heap object
whose isa is bad, so it is NOT on the roster. Task #227.

